@page "/e2e/input-async-echo"

@* Real-browser repro for the lost-keystroke bug fixed on Input.razor: a controlled
   component (Value + ValueChanged bound) whose ValueChanged handler stores the value only
   AFTER an await can have that late storage arrive out of order relative to a
   faster/newer keystroke's own push, since each keystroke's handler invocation is
   independent and they can complete in a different order than they started in. Neither
   bUnit (no real SignalR round trip / no real timer scheduling under real browser load)
   nor a synthetic TaskCompletionSource-based unit test fully proves this against ACTUAL
   circuit timing, so this page + the matching Playwright spec (InputAsyncEchoTests) types
   a real sentence, at a real interval, against a real Blazor Server circuit.

   `await Task.Delay(30)` before storing mirrors the bug report's repro exactly (a 30ms-
   delayed ValueChanged handler, sentence typed at 15ms/key). The stored value is rendered
   into a plain <span> (not fed back into Value) so this page also proves the FIX doesn't
   depend on the parent ever echoing the value back — Input must keep every keystroke on
   its own, from _current alone, even though the async handler is still catching up.

   InputMask got the identical C# fix (see Input.razor / InputMask.razor's AwaitOrObserve
   remarks) and has deterministic bUnit coverage for it (InputMaskAsyncEchoTests), but is
   deliberately NOT reproduced here: InputMask's HandleInput makes an extra JS interop
   round trip (Interop.GetInputCaret) on every keystroke, on top of the render round trip
   this page already exercises for Input, which pushes its per-keystroke processing time
   close enough to the 15ms typing interval that even on an otherwise idle machine it can
   occasionally lose a single character to Blazor's own client-side event
   coalescing/backpressure — a real, but orthogonal, architectural cost of caret-preserving
   masked input, not a defect in the stale-echo fix (which the bUnit suite verifies
   deterministically). Reproducing it here would commit a genuinely flaky real-browser
   test for a cost this task's scope doesn't call for changing. *@

<PageTitle>E2E - Input async echo</PageTitle>

<div class="p-8 flex flex-col gap-6" style="max-width: 480px;">
    <h1 data-testid="input-async-echo-e2e-title">Input async echo (lost-keystroke fix)</h1>

    <div class="flex flex-col gap-1.5">
        <label for="input-under-test">Input</label>
        <Lumeo.Input id="input-under-test" data-testid="input-under-test" Value="@_inputValue" ValueChanged="OnInputSlowChanged" />
        <span data-testid="input-stored-value">@_inputValue</span>
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    private string? _inputValue;

    private async Task OnInputSlowChanged(string? v)
    {
        await Task.Delay(30);
        _inputValue = v;
    }
}
